 -   **When to use**: Suitable for real-time rendering of participating media where single scattering dominates (e.g., fog, smoke, thin clouds) and volumetric shadows are desired [S1, unofficial].
 -   **When not to use**: Not suitable for dense media where multiple scattering is significant, or when full global illumination is required [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The Single Scattering Illumination model was introduced in the paper "Single Scattering Effects for Computer Games" [S1, unofficial]. The work focuses on real-time hardware rendering of volumetric lighting effects, specifically introducing the concept of indirect single scattering (ISS) alongside direct single scattering (DSS) [S1, unofficial]. The method uses an angular formulation of the radiative transfer equation and precomputed 2D tables to enable efficient computation [S1, unofficial]. It is implemented in frameworks like VStudio for interactive volume visualization [S2].
